## How do you give a busy student café a home outside Instagram?

Klub Gama is the student club of the Faculties of Civil Engineering, Architecture and Mechanical Engineering in Niš, a café a few steps from the lecture halls where students get coffee, breakfast and a seat between classes. It was busy every morning, but online it existed only as an Instagram profile. I built a bilingual website with a page-turning e-menu, a 27-photo gallery, an events page and the opening hours always one tap away, so anyone who hears the name can find out when it is open and what it serves in a few seconds.

## Why was Instagram not enough?

Instagram is good at mood and bad at facts. The questions a new student actually has are simple: is it open right now, what is on the menu, where exactly is it, and can I call. On a profile those answers are scattered across old posts and highlights, if they exist at all.

A website puts them in a fixed place. It also appears when someone types the name into Google, which a profile does not do reliably. Instagram still matters for daily updates, so the site links to it rather than trying to replace it.

## How did I structure the site?

The homepage follows the order a visitor decides in. A full-width slider opens on the room itself, with a short line about coffee, breakfast and company near the amphitheatre. Below it come the story of the club, the faculties it belongs to, the menu, a gallery preview, reviews from students, and finally the contact block with hours, the address and a map.

The phone number sits in the header on every page and is tappable on a phone, because calling is the fastest way to ask about a table or an event. A red side tab opens a panel with upcoming events without leaving the page you are on.

Two more pages carry the detail. The gallery holds 27 photos of the space in a gap-free grid that opens into a full-screen viewer. The events page groups everything by month, and each card in the side panel links straight to its event there.

## Why a menu you can flip like a book?

Most cafés either skip the menu online or upload a PDF that nobody opens on a phone. I wanted something that felt like picking up the printed menu at the table, so the menu is an e-menu book: the cover carries the club logo and the pages turn with a real page-flip effect.

Under the effect it is still ordinary data. Every item lives in one file, so changing a dish or a price is one edit, and the book rebuilds itself from it. No PDF, no reprinting, and nothing a visitor has to download.

## Why build it in two languages?

Niš has exchange students, visiting professors and guests at faculty events who do not read Serbian. The site runs in Serbian by default with a full English version behind the EN switch in the header. Navigation, the menu, the gallery, events and the opening hours all exist in both languages, and every piece of text comes from one place keyed by language, so the two versions cannot drift apart.

## What was the design approach?

The look is warm and a little industrial, matching the room: espresso brown, caramel and cream, with Oswald for the bold headings, Open Sans for reading and Merriweather italic for the lines that carry the voice. The layout is boxed, with the photography doing most of the work.

Motion is there but kept in service of the content. The hero slider has a light parallax, sections reveal as you scroll, and the partner logos run in a slow marquee. On a phone the hero is sharper, the call button is bigger and the gallery tiles fade in at full size, because most visitors arrive on mobile.

## What was the tech stack?

The site is built with Astro, which ships almost no JavaScript by default, so it loads quickly even on a phone on mobile data. GSAP handles the reveals and the slider, and a small page-flip library powers the e-menu. Every photo is served as WebP. The same lightweight approach runs through [Porta Brzi Brod](/blog/porta-brzi-brod/) and [Zvonačka Banja](/blog/zvonacka-banja/), and if you are weighing a build like this against a ready-made theme, my guide to [choosing a custom website or a template](/blog/custom-website-vs-template/) covers where each one makes sense.

## What was the result?

The first version went live in two days, with the English version added afterwards. Klub Gama now has a site that answers the four questions every new visitor has, open hours, menu, location and how to call, on the first screen they reach. The menu feels like the real thing instead of a file to download, the gallery shows the room to people who have never been inside, and the club controls all of it from a handful of data files rather than depending on what a social feed happens to show.
